WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.645
A score is only as good as the data behind it.

2
00:00:02.925 --> 00:00:10.904
In week 4 you will put a number on your fine-tuned model: accuracy on the extraction task, side by side with a frontier model.

3
00:00:11.184 --> 00:00:14.192
That number will drive a decision about cost and quality.

4
00:00:14.472 --> 00:00:30.216
This lesson is about the ways that number can be wrong before any model runs, because the data that produced it was flawed: duplicates that let the model see test answers in training, related documents split across train and test, labels that are themselves mistakes,

5
00:00:30.496 --> 00:00:33.013
or a test set that doesn’t look like production.

6
00:00:33.293 --> 00:00:34.765
None of this is new to you.

7
00:00:35.045 --> 00:00:40.784
Studio 3 built test sets, kept test cases out of prompts, and put error bars on pass rates.

8
00:00:41.064 --> 00:00:51.795
Fine-tuning raises the stakes, because now the same pool of labeled data feeds both training and evaluation, and anything that crosses from one to the other inflates the score.

9
00:00:52.075 --> 00:01:06.219
Kapoor and Narayanan (2022) surveyed machine-learning-based science and found leakage errors affecting 329 papers across 17 fields, often producing overly optimistic results.

10
00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:10.659
Careful researchers made these mistakes; you will be tempted to make them too.
